# Claude Code. CLAUDE.md and the project knowledge under .claude/ — the seven
# skills, the three subagents, and jaos-measure's three scripts — ARE committed.
# They are the project's and not one machine's, CLAUDE.md routes to them by
# name, and geomean.py is what enforces D46 (a geometric mean of per-instance
# ratios, never a total over a set). A clone that lacks them gets instructions
# pointing at tooling that is not there.
#
# Two files stay out, for different reasons.
#
# settings.local.json sets defaultMode: bypassPermissions. That is a permission
# posture chosen for this machine; committing it would silently impose "run
# without asking" on anyone who clones this repository and opens it in Claude
# Code. It is also the file Claude Code designates as the personal override.
/.claude/settings.local.json
#
# scheduled_tasks.lock is a session id, a pid and a timestamp, rewritten on
# every run — pure diff churn, and it says which machine ran what and when.
/.claude/scheduled_tasks.lock

# Build output
/build/

# Test instances are fetched and checksum-verified, never committed
# (PLAN 2.10). bench/netlib.manifest is what pins them; the files
# themselves stay out, licences and size both.
/bench/instances/
/bench/instances-kennington/
/bench/instances-infeas/

# Acceptance results ARE committed (PLAN 2.9): every figure in them —
# status, objective, iterations, work units — is deterministic (D8), so the
# record reproduces on any machine and a diff of it is the history of what
# the solver did to the reference set. No wall-clock number is in there to
# make it host-specific (D17).

# Competitor solvers are fetched, checksum-verified and built at dev time,
# never committed — the same rule the instances follow. bench/compare/
# solvers.manifest is what pins them.
/bench/compare/solvers/

# HiGHS writes this into the working directory when it runs.
/Highs.log
